A1. These have great borrowing powers. ~ NEIGHBORS
A9. The kind of feet Paul Whiteman seems to have. ~ CUBIC
A13. This is what your savings will do—if you don't weaken. ~ ACCRUE
A14. Nothing but New York capital here. ~ ALBANY
A16. The kind of nonsense that is easily seen through. ~ SHEER
A17. What a man does when he puts himself in a child's place. ~ EATS
A18. This is in wrong. ~ ON
A19. Brilliant flashes from the diamond. ~ HOMERS
A21. This describes an unknown age. ~ HER
A22. What every miner has to go through. ~ ADIT
A24. This man belonged to one of the first families. ~ ADAM
A25. They must have got this out of shape. ~ APE
A26. Use this when they get rough. ~ FILE
A27. This never crows, yet produces things by the dozen. ~ HEN
A29. Damages, ~ HARMS
A31. They've tried many rackets to put things over this. ~ NET
A32. This doesn't get you very far in arithmetic. ~ AR
A33. A side issue for Adam's express company. ~ RIB
A34. This was done in the past. ~ DO
A35. Sun and cocoa butter. ~ TAN
A37. To tergiversate. ~ LIE
A39. Figuratively speaking this carries more weight. ~ STOUTER
A41. This will go all around the Japs. ~ OBI
A42. A summer resort for the barn-yard folks. ~ LEA
A43. This is in the middle of Alaska just now. ~ AS
A44. This despises anything catty. ~ RAT
A45. This always starts last. ~ LA
A46. Some think this country home is an eyesore. ~ STY
A48. This old bird should never have shown her tail feathers. ~ EGRET
A50. This is pretty smooth and will try to slip over anything. ~ EEL
A51. A villainous look. ~ LEER
A53. You are lucky if you get this part of your share. ~ ARE
A54. You have to be air-minded to get this word. ~ SOAR
A56. The way a rhinoceros looks. ~ UGLY
A57. This was backed up by Raskob himself. ~ CAR
A58. Many like this used to hang around castles. ~ TURRET
A61. This might be somebody's husband. ~ JO
A62. These are dear things. ~ SIRS
A64. This has always been opposed to poetry. ~ PROSE
A65. The latest kind of cars. ~ STREET
A67. What a tourist does for home comfort. ~ YEARNS
A68. This used to have a lot of pull until Gen. Motors took possession. ~ HORSE
A69. This is what high finance does to Amos 'n' Andy. ~ DEPRESSES

D1. We parked this car vertically. (It takes much less space.) ~ NASH
D2. A recall. ~ ECHO
D3. The cook turned this one down cold when General Electric came along. ~ ICEMAN
D4. What every miser has. ~ GREED
D5. What a man is apt to say when his mother-in-law leaves. ~ HURRAH
D6. This is 50 per cent. beer. ~ BE
D7. A shining light in historical Egypt. ~ RA
D8. What a man usually does when he has tire trouble. ~ SLEEPS
D9. A bird fancier. ~ CAT
D10. What Main Street is becoming. ~ UNSAFE
D11. This is where the bird flew. ~ BY
D12. A reference to the 100 years that are the hardest. ~ CENTENNIAL
D15. This is the way the college students of today are headed. ~ BARE
D18. This has been standard for a long time. ~ OIL
D20. What a slap in the face does. ~ SMARTS
D21. Kitchen cleaner. ~ HAMBURGER
D23. This is the same as before. ~ DITTO
D27. Religious whoopee. ~ HALLELUJAH
D28. This has always been in a wet state. ~ ERIE
D30. A Spanish river. ~ RIO
D31. This will follow neither. ~ NOR
D34. This is what dogs do to eats. ~ DETEST
D36. I'll say this is clever. ~ ABLE
D38. This must have been a frame-up for the artists' colony. ~ EASEL
D39. The way to address a stranger. ~ SAY
D40. This is black and viscous and often "sticks 'em up." ~ TAR
D47. Meetings not to be revealed. ~ TRYSTS
D48. What you did when you changed your mind. ~ ERASED
D49. Those who put this over are shylocks. ~ TOUPEE
D50. Flaws in a diamond. ~ ERRORS
D52. Nom. I object.—me. ~ EGO
D53. This is one farm measure that is really on the square. ~ ACRE
D55. A town in France noted for its hangings. ~ ARRAS
D59. This is a literal slave to cross-word puzzles. ~ ESNE
D60. The name of a girl that got all twisted. ~ TESS
D63. You never find this in a cheerful mood. ~ IRE
D65. This abbreviation was put in for several reasons. ~ SR
D66. The beginning of temptation. ~ TE
D67. A short period of time. ~ YR


